WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
The first film classified bacteria and viruses by what they can do.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.660
If both carry genes, why can a bacterium grow while a virus waits?

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.500
Open them and follow the work.

4
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:18.783
Start with the bacterium, a prokaryotic cell.

5
00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:22.543
Its membrane controls what enters and leaves.

6
00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:25.543
Cytoplasm fills the inside.

7
00:00:27.043 --> 00:00:30.183
Ribosomes build proteins from instructions in DNA.

8
00:00:31.603 --> 00:00:35.583
That DNA sits in a nucleoid, a region without a membrane.

9
00:00:36.683 --> 00:00:42.063
Many bacteria also have a cell wall that helps prevent bursting when water enters.

10
00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:44.643
Some have a capsule outside.

11
00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:48.863
Some have flagella that spin to push them through water.

12
00:00:50.143 --> 00:00:52.583
These parts vary, but the working cell remains.

13
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.153
Now open the virus.

14
00:00:56.513 --> 00:01:00.313
Its DNA or RNA sits inside a protein capsid.

15
00:01:01.493 --> 00:01:03.933
Some viruses add a lipid envelope outside.

16
00:01:05.313 --> 00:01:08.393
There is no cytoplasm and no ribosome workbench.

17
00:01:09.613 --> 00:01:12.053
A host cell must build every viral protein.

18
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:16.453
Genes are the shared part, not proof that both are cells.

19
00:01:17.773 --> 00:01:20.913
The bacterium reads its genes with its own ribosomes.

20
00:01:22.393 --> 00:01:26.193
Viral genes can be read only after they reach a suitable host cell.

21
00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:30.157
A working cell also needs energy and carbon.

22
00:01:31.497 --> 00:01:32.797
Energy powers the work.

23
00:01:34.297 --> 00:01:36.097
Carbon supplies material for new parts.

24
00:01:37.357 --> 00:01:42.637
For a useful first model, sort bacteria into common patterns by where those inputs

25
00:01:42.637 --> 00:01:43.637
come from.

26
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:48.587
Heterotrophs take in carbon compounds for both energy and carbon.

27
00:01:50.327 --> 00:01:54.447
Photoautotrophs use light for energy and carbon dioxide for carbon.

28
00:01:56.147 --> 00:02:00.487
Chemoautotrophs use chemicals for energy and carbon dioxide for carbon.

29
00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.687
Predict the pattern in a dark spring.

30
00:02:04.907 --> 00:02:10.127
The bacterium uses sulfur compounds for energy and carbon dioxide for carbon.

31
00:02:11.347 --> 00:02:13.687
Which name fits, and which input tells you?

32
00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:21.163
Chemoautotroph fits.

33
00:02:22.743 --> 00:02:26.863
Chemical energy selects chemo, and carbon dioxide supplies the carbon.

34
00:02:28.063 --> 00:02:29.843
The model is a simplification.

35
00:02:31.263 --> 00:02:36.263
Energy source and carbon source are separate questions, and a few bacteria mix the

36
00:02:36.263 --> 00:02:37.263
patterns.

37
00:02:37.733 --> 00:02:40.873
Dependence alone does not make a cell nonliving.

38
00:02:42.153 --> 00:02:47.353
Some bacteria grow only inside other cells, yet keep ribosomes and metabolism.

39
00:02:48.993 --> 00:02:53.573
Viruses remain different because they are not cells and have no ribosomes of their

40
00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:54.573
own.

41
00:02:54.917 --> 00:02:57.117
Size does not decide the case either.

42
00:02:58.457 --> 00:03:01.237
Most viruses are smaller than typical bacteria.

43
00:03:02.517 --> 00:03:07.297
But some giant viruses are longer than small bacteria, so that shortcut breaks.

44
00:03:08.717 --> 00:03:10.557
Parts and work matter more than length.

45
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.650
The comparison now has a reason.

46
00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.490
Bacterial parts let the cell obtain inputs and build proteins.

47
00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.070
Viral parts carry genes between hosts.

48
00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:27.870
Bacteria are living cells; viruses are not cells and are classed nonliving.
